From 8810edefcba9e11339ba6cfc04a5810f31c0e345 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Denys Fedoryshchenko <denys.f@collabora.com>
Date: Wed, 27 May 2026 12:50:13 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] boringssl: Fix C23 memchr const-correctness in C wrapper

Modern glibc versions (2.43+) define memchr as a generic macro under
C23 to preserve const qualification. When s is const void *, the
result of memchr(s, c, n) is typed const void *, which discards
qualifiers when returned from OPENSSL_memchr (which returns void *).

Add an explicit cast to (void *) to remain compatible with both C23
and older standards, matching the approach taken by AWS-LC.

This patch can be dropped on the next boringssl uprev.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [superseded by upstream C++-only header migration, e.g. https://boringssl-review.googlesource.com/c/boringssl/+/87847]
---

diff --git a/crypto/internal.h b/crypto/internal.h
index a77102d..badff4b 100644
--- a/crypto/internal.h
+++ b/crypto/internal.h
@@ -1024,7 +1024,8 @@ static inline void *OPENSSL_memchr(const void *s, int c, size_t n) {
     return NULL;
   }
 
-  return memchr(s, c, n);
+  // C23 makes memchr const-correct, returning const void *.
+  return (void *)memchr(s, c, n);
 }
 
 #endif  // __cplusplus
